The board state has four legal columns for player x: 0, 1, 5 and 6. Columns 1 and 5 are immediate losses. Placing in either column fills the cell that unlocks the already-present open three of o-pieces on row 2; o then occupies the newly-exposed cell on row 2 and completes four-in-a-row horizontally.

Column 0 creates no threat. Column 6, however, immediately creates a threat: the placement occupies (3,6) and leaves the open three of x-pieces on row 3 ready to become four-in-a-row if x is later allowed to occupy (3,5). Consequently o is forced to answer in column 5.

The forced continuation is then unique:

- o’s block at (3,5) unlocks o’s own open three on row 2, so x must answer at (2,5);
- that placement unlocks x’s open three on row 1, so o must answer at (1,5);
- x can now occupy (2,6), producing three consecutive x-pieces vertically in column 6 and thereby threatening a win at (1,6);
- o is forced to block at (1,6).

After these exchanges both of x’s original open threes and o’s original open three have been neutralised. The only remaining empty cells lie on row 0 (plus a few cells that can be shown by exhaustive enumeration of the four-cell windows to be incapable of producing a new four-in-a-row). Optimal play from that point therefore fills the board without a winner.

Thus column 6 is the unique move that forces a draw; every other legal move either loses at once or permits o to force at least the same draw while retaining more freedom. Hence the optimal choice is column 6.

Final Answer: 6

